{"name":"Petit Four","hex":"#7fbad1","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"petit four","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small cake generally eaten at the end of a meal or served as part of a large buffet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/petit_four"}],"entries":[{"title":"Petit four","blocks":[{"text":"A petit four is a small bite-sized confectionery, pâtisserie or savory appetiser. The name is French for \"small oven\". Certain petit fours are also known as mignardises. In England they are also known as fancies, and dainties in the prairie provinces of Canada."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Petits.fours.wmt.jpg/330px-Petits.fours.wmt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petit_four","wikidata":"Q940757"},{"title":"Petit","blocks":[{"text":"Petit is a French-language surname literally meaning \"small\" or \"little\". Notable people with the surname include:"},{"items":["Adriana Petit, Spanish multidisciplinary artist","Alexis Thérèse Petit (1791–1820), French physicist","Amandine Petit, French model, beauty pageant titleholder, and Miss France 2021","Antoine Petit (1722–1794), French physician","Antoni Martí Petit, prime minister of Andorra","Cavelier Petit, American politician","François Pourfour du Petit (1664–1741), French anatomist","Gabrielle Petit (1893–1916), Belgian spy","Gabrielle Petit (feminist) (1860–1952), French feminist activist, anticlerical, libertarian socialist, newspaper editor","Henriette Petit (1894-1983), Chilean painter","Jean-Martin Petit (1772–1856), French General during the Napoleonic Wars","Luis Romero Petit (1917–2017), Venezuelan baseball player","Monique Ruck-Petit, Swiss and French chess master","Paul Petit (aviator) (1890-1918), French flying ace","Paul Petit, French racing driver","Philippe Petit, French high-wire artist","Pierre Petit (photographer) (1832–1909), French pho